+{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_015sLUVygPuhQBbJyPCr8xUb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture of what needs to be implemented. Let me create the CONTRACT.md and then implement all 8 plugins.\n\nLet me analyze each plugin's requirements from the tests:\n\n## 1. markdown-ext\n- `createMarkdownExtPlugin()` factory\n- `transformContent` hook\n- Converts markdown to HTML\n- Handles footnotes: `[^1]` → `<sup><a href=\"#fn-1\">1</a></sup>`\n- Footnote section: `<section class=\"footnotes\">` with `<li id=\"fn-1\">text</li>`\n\n\n- Admonitions: `:::note\\ncontent\\n:::` → `<div class=\"admonition admonition-note\">content</div>` for note, warning, and tip types\n- Preserves existing HTML and handles empty content\n\n## 2. taxonomy\n- `createTaxonomyPlugin()` factory\n- `beforeBuild` hook that builds a tag index and stores it in `ctx.data.get('taxonomy-tags')` as a map of tags to content files\n- Tags are normalized to lowercase\n- `afterBuild` hook that generates tag pages at `tags/{tag}.html` with file titles\n- Handles files with no tags and empty tag lists\n\n## 3. sitemap\n- `createSitemapPlugin()` factory with an `afterBuild` hook\n- Generates `sitemap.xml` in the output directory with all content page URLs, taxonomy tag page URLs, and the feed.xml URL if it exists\n- Uses proper XML structure with version declaration and urlset elements\n- Handles sites with no tag pages\n\n## 4. syntax-highlight\n- `createSyntaxHighlightPlugin()` factory with a `transformContent` hook\n- Adds the `highlighted` class to code blocks and `data-language` attributes to pre tags based on the language class\n- Leaves inline code tags untouched and skips code blocks without a language class\n\n## 5. search-index\n- `createSearchIndexPlugin()` factory that runs after the build\n- Creates a `search-index.json` file containing an array of entries with title, slug, plain text content, and tags\n- Strips HTML from code blocks so they're still searchable\n\n## 6. dev-server\n- `createDevServerPlugin()` factory storing configuration in `ctx.data` with port, routes, asset paths, and search index path settings\n- Pulls asset paths from the asset manifest and creates routes for serving assets and static HTML pages\n- Falls back gracefully if the asset pipeline data is missing\n\n## 7. rss-feed\n- `createRssFeedPlugin()` factory generating a valid RSS 2.0 feed with items wrapped in CDATA\n- Includes category tags from the taxonomy...\n\nWriting RSS feed structure...\n\nI need to verify the marked v17 API since the syntax for parsing markdown might differ from what I'm assuming. Let me check whether `marked.parse()` works synchronously or if I need to handle it differently in this version.\n\nFor the markdown-ext plugin, I'll need to manually implement footnotes and admonitions since marked doesn't have built-in support for these features. The footnotes should render as superscript links with a corresponding footnotes section, and admonitions should be styled divs with the appropriate class names based on the admonition type.\n\nImplementing the plugin... Writing plugin code...\n\nActually, I need to reconsider the marked API—in the latest version, `marked.parse()` is synchronous by default, so I don't need to make the transform function async. Let me verify the exact behavior for the current marked version to ensure compatibility.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the test case for footnotes more carefully.\n\nI need to handle two scenarios: a single footnote reference and multiple footnote references. For each, I'm checking that the inline references render as superscript links with the correct IDs, and that the footnotes section at the end contains list items with matching IDs and the footnote content.\n\nMy strategy is to preprocess the markdown by extracting footnote definitions, stripping them from the text, converting the reference syntax to HTML, then letting marked handle the rest before appending the footnotes section.\n\nThe tricky part is that marked doesn't natively support footnotes, so I need to replace `[^n]` with HTML before it processes the text—that way marked will preserve the HTML I've inserted. I also need to handle admonitions the same way, processing them before marked runs. Writing the asset pipeline plugin... Writing the syntax-highlight plugin...\n\nNow I'm building a taxonomy plugin that collects files by their tags during the build process, normalizing them to lowercase and storing the mapping in the build context for later use.\n\nWriting plugin code... Building the RSS items...\n\nNow I'm assembling the complete RSS feed structure with the channel metadata and items, then writing it to the output as feed.xml. Let me double-check the pubDate handling—the test expects that undefined dates don't produce a pubDate tag, and my conditional approach handles that correctly. Next I need to work on the search-index plugin. Writing test cases... I'm verifying the whitespace handling in my regex replacements—stripping HTML tags and collapsing multiple spaces should give me clean content like `'Some text here'` without extra spacing. For code blocks embedded in HTML, the same approach extracts just the code content, and since the test uses `toContain()` rather than exact matching, minor spacing variations won't cause issues. Now I'm moving on to implement the sitemap plugin...\n\nWriting sitemap plugin... I'm checking the test cases to understand how the sitemap should handle different scenarios. When there are no tag pages in the output, the sitemap correctly excludes them, and when tag pages exist as regular output entries, they get picked up automatically since I'm iterating over all output keys. Now I'm looking at the dev-server plugin implementation...\n\nBuilding the dev server configuration... Looking at the test for content routes, I need to ensure that HTML files in the output get converted to static routes, and the test expects exactly two of them when there are two HTML files present.\n\nFor the marked library import, I'm checking the v17 API—the synchronous `marked.parse(text)` should return a string directly, so I'll cast it to ensure TypeScript is satisfied.\n\nNow I need to create the individual plugin files in `src/plugins/` since the tests import from specific paths like `../../src/plugins/markdown-ext.js`. I'll also set up an index file for the plugins directory, and make sure the ESM imports work correctly with the Node16 module configuration.\n\nFor the `marked.parse()` function, I need to handle the async behavior carefully since marked v17 might return either a string or Promise depending on the version. I'll use `await` to safely handle both cases in the async transform method.\n\nNow I'm ready to implement all the plugin files, starting with the admonition regex pattern to match the triple-colon syntax with content between them. I'm tracing through the processing pipeline for both admonitions and footnotes to verify they work correctly. For admonitions, the regex replacement converts the container syntax into a div, which marked should preserve as a block-level HTML element. For footnotes, I'm extracting the reference definitions and then processing the remaining content through marked. The multi-footnote test is working as expected—I'm extracting multiple footnote definitions, replacing each reference with the appropriate superscript link, and then appending all of them to the footnotes section in the correct order.\n\nI'm also verifying that my regex only captures numeric footnotes, which matches what the tests require, and that the Map maintains insertion order so the footnotes render in sequence. The footnote definition removal handles blank lines properly, and marked preserves the inline HTML I'm injecting for the superscript links within paragraphs.\n\nNow I'm planning out the plugin file structure across multiple modules for markdown extensions, asset handling, syntax highlighting, taxonomy, RSS feeds, search indexing, sitemaps, and a dev server, plus an index file for re-exports. I also need to ensure strict TypeScript compliance throughout with proper typing and undefined handling.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14609,"cache_read_input_tokens":23826,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14609},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"51b680eb-f5dd-4ab8-aead-1b26c00c64ac","uuid":"34005454-74e4-4746-bd58-a2ec19a95009","request_id":"req_011CbZmEmRJvQGmGEXqo7DBY"}
